/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"production-level-9","number":9,"title":"Module 9 — Arrangement Ruler","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Navigate, zoom, and loop the linear timeline using standardized shortcuts. - Scaffold sectional form using explicit ruler Locators.","modules":[{"id":"production-m-9-1","code":"9","title":"Section B: Arrangement as a Song Instrument","blurb":"- Navigate, zoom, and loop the linear timeline using standardized shortcuts. - Scaffold sectional form using explicit ruler Locators.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-13","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 9 — Section B: Arrangement as a Song Instrument","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Master the linear timeline as an independent compositional instrument. Author a complete, original piece of sectional music written directly on the horizontal ruler from Bar 1 left-to-right. Master multi-take Take Lanes comping, linked-track phase coherence, global timeline manipulation commands, and psychoacoustic energy automation curves. Attain <strong>Gate D Certification</strong>.   <strong>Required Core Module.</strong> Gate D.   <strong>Strict Pedagogical Invariant:</strong> You may NOT drag, record, or duplicate Deliverable 1 (Session) onto the ruler and submit it as Deliverable 2. Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual (§7.5) establishes that Session clips and Arrangement clips exist in two separate, independent pools. Deliverable 2 must be an entirely original composition.   <strong>Primary Source References:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual (Ch. 6: Arrangement View, Ch. 7.5: Session/Arrangement Independence, Ch. 19: Recording, Ch. 21: Comping, Ch. 25: Automation); Sound on Sound (<em>Ableton Live: Session &amp; Arrangement Views</em>); Beat Kitchen (Ch. 6: <em>Arrangement and Energy Management</em>); <em>Music-Production-Songwriting-Knowledgebase.md</em> (§1.1, §1.2, §8.7, §9.3–9.5); <em>The Ultimate Guide to Build-ups.pdf</em>; <em>The Art of Turnarounds_ 9 Tricks for Better Transitions in EDM.pdf</em>; <em>The Advanced Guide to Tension and Energy in Electronic Music.pdf</em>; <em>PML - Guide to Electronic Arrangements.pdf</em>; <em>Arrangement Checklist.pdf</em>."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 9.1 — The Ruler: Navigation, Locators, and Timeline Commands","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section ID:</strong> <code>production-m9-1</code>   <strong>Learning Outcomes:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Navigate, zoom, and loop the linear timeline using standardized shortcuts.","Scaffold sectional form using explicit ruler Locators.","Execute metric changes and tempo automation across section boundaries.","Apply global Time commands (<code>Insert Silence</code>, <code>Delete Time</code>, <code>Duplicate Time</code>, <code>Cut Time</code>) across multi-track arrangements.","Execute Linked-Track multi-channel editing and equal-power crossfades.","Harvest linear blocks into Session scenes via <code>Consolidate Time to New Scene</code>.","Construct a 32-bar linear timeline proof and achieve <strong>Gate D Certification</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-1","title":"9.1.1 — Layout, Zooming Mechanics, and the Arrangement Loop Brace","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.1 — Layout, Zooming Mechanics, and the Arrangement Loop Brace","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Master horizontal timeline navigation, adaptive grid scaling, and loop brace manipulation.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Linear Topology of Arrangement View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Arrangement View represents musical time along a continuous horizontal axis, flowing from left (t=0, Bar 1.1.1) to right. Tracks are stacked vertically, with signal processing flowing horizontally inside the bottom Device View and audio summing into the Master Bus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">graph LR\n    subgraph Live 12 Standard Arrangement Topology\n        Ruler[\"Beat-Time Ruler (Bars : Beats : Sixteenths)\"]\n        T1[\"Track 1: DRUMS (MIDI / Drum Rack)\"]\n        T2[\"Track 2: BASS (MIDI / Drift)\"]\n        T3[\"Track 3: KEYS (MIDI / Electric)\"]\n        T4[\"Track 4: AUDIO_VOCAL (Take Lanes)\"]\n        Master[\"Master Bus (POW-r 3 / SPAN / Limiter)\"]\n        \n        Ruler --&gt; T1\n        Ruler --&gt; T2\n        Ruler --&gt; T3\n        Ruler --&gt; T4\n        T1 --&gt; Master\n        T2 --&gt; Master\n        T3 --&gt; Master\n        T4 --&gt; Master\n    end</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. View Toggling and Navigation Shortcuts","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Toggle Views (<code>Tab</code>)</strong>: Switches focus instantly between Session View and Arrangement View.","<strong>Vertical Track Zoom (<code>Alt + Mouse Wheel</code> or <code>Alt + + / -</code>)</strong>: Expands track lane heights to inspect Take Lanes and waveform transient peaks.","<strong>Horizontal Time Zoom (<code>Mouse Drag on Ruler</code> or <code>+ / -</code> keys)</strong>:","<strong>Zoom to Selection (<code>Z</code>)</strong>: Zooms the arrangement display to fit the currently highlighted time region precisely across the screen.","<strong>Zoom Out Full Overview (<code>Shift + Z</code>)</strong>: Instantly resets the zoom level to display the entire timeline from Bar 1 to the final decaying reverb tail.","<strong>Follow Song (<code>F</code> or <code>Ctrl + Shift + F</code>)</strong>: Locks timeline scrolling to follow the playhead in real time."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. The Arrangement Loop Brace (Ctrl + L)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Arrangement Loop Brace isolates a specific bar range for rehearsal, recording, or sectional editing:"},{"type":"list","items":["Highlight a region of time across one or more tracks (e.g., Bars 9.1.1 to 17.1.1).","Press <strong><code>Ctrl + L</code></strong>. Live automatically moves and sets the Loop Brace boundaries to match the selection exactly.","When the Loop Toggle switch in the Control Bar is active (yellow), playback will loop indefinitely within the brace.","Drag the loop header to shift the active loop window across adjacent 8-bar sections without changing the loop length."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Create an empty Live Set, toggle to Arrangement View (<code>Tab</code>), place a 4-bar MIDI clip at Bar 9, select it, press <code>Z</code>, press <code>Ctrl + L</code>, and press <code>Shift + Z</code>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-2","title":"9.1.2 — Locators: Pinning Structural Milestones to the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.2 — Locators: Pinning Structural Milestones to the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Anchor sectional boundaries using named timeline Locators for navigation and arrangement scaffolding.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Structural Scaffolding on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A Locator is a permanent navigation bookmark pinned to a specific downbeat on the Beat-Time Ruler. In professional linear production, you <strong>never write audio without locators</strong>. Locators define the macro-structural roadmap before a single note is recorded."}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Locator Creation and Management","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Add Locator</strong>: Right-click in the scrub area directly below the Beat-Time Ruler and select <strong>Add Locator</strong>, or click the <strong>Set</strong> button in the top-right Control Bar (or assign a custom key command).","<strong>Naming Protocol (<code>Ctrl + R</code>)</strong>:","<code>01_Intro</code> (Bar 1.1.1)","<code>02_Verse_1</code> (Bar 9.1.1)","<code>03_Pre_Chorus_1</code> (Bar 25.1.1)","<code>04_Chorus_1</code> (Bar 33.1.1)","<code>05_Verse_2</code> (Bar 49.1.1)","<code>06_Bridge_Breakdown</code> (Bar 65.1.1)","<code>07_Final_Chorus</code> (Bar 81.1.1)","<code>08_Outro</code> (Bar 105.1.1)","<strong>Locator Navigation (<code>Prev / Next Locator</code> buttons)</strong>: Pressing the <code>|&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;|</code> buttons beside the Set button jumps the playhead to the previous or next locator in quantization sync (governed by the Global Quantization menu)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Locator Cueing and Rehearsal","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking any Locator during arrangement playback commands Live to jump to that section at the next bar boundary. This allows rapid structural testing: jumping from Verse 1 directly to Chorus 1 to verify that the energy jump feels earned and natural."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Open an empty project, insert 8 locators matching the standard ABABCB pop roadmap, and verify that clicking each locator jumps the playhead cleanly on the beat."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-3","title":"9.1.3 — Time Signature and Tempo Changes on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.3 — Time Signature and Tempo Changes on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Automate metric changes and tempo curves along the horizontal timeline.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Metric Changes on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Linear composition is not restricted to a static 4/4 meter. In advanced pop, progressive electronic, and film cue writing, inserting an isolated 2/4 or 3/4 bar before a chorus creates intense rhythmic anticipation:"},{"type":"list","items":["Right-click the scrub area at the target bar (e.g., Bar 24.1.1, the final bar of a Pre-Chorus).","Select <strong>Insert Time Signature Change</strong>.","Type <code>2/4</code>. Live inserts a 2-beat measure.","Right-click at Bar 25.1.1 (the Chorus downbeat)  <strong>Insert Time Signature Change</strong>  Type <code>4/4</code>.","<em>Result</em>: A 2-beat turn creates a sudden, urgent downbeat arrival that elevates chorus impact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Master Track Tempo Automation","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Unfold the <strong>Master Track</strong> lane at the bottom of Arrangement View.","Select <strong>Mixer</strong> in the device chooser, and <strong>Song Tempo</strong> in the parameter chooser.","Draw tempo ramps:","For an accelerating electronic buildup: Draw an exponential ramp climbing from 124.0 BPM to 128.0 BPM across Bars 25–32.","For an organic acoustic ritardando: Draw a linear deceleration from 120.0 BPM to 112.0 BPM across the final 4 bars of the Outro."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Automate a 2-beat 2/4 measure on Bar 16, followed by a return to 4/4 on Bar 17, and observe how the Arrangement grid adapts."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-4","title":"9.1.4 — The Four Global Time Commands","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.4 — The Four Global Time Commands","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Manipulate horizontal song structure across all tracks simultaneously using global Time commands.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Global Structural Surgery","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When an arrangement is 80 bars long with 12 active tracks, dragging individual clips with the mouse to insert a new 8-bar section introduces massive human error (misaligned clips, broken automation envelopes, phase shifts). Live 12 provides four <strong>Global Time Commands</strong> that operate across all tracks simultaneously:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Global Time Command","Keyboard Shortcut","Operational Mechanism","Common Production Use Case"],"rows":[["<strong>Insert Silence</strong>","<strong><code>Ctrl + I</code></strong>","Opens a dialogue requesting bar/beat length. Inserts empty timeline space across ALL tracks at the playhead, pushing all subsequent clips, locators, and automation to the right.","Adding an 8-bar Bridge after Chorus 2 without manually moving downstream clips."],["<strong>Delete Time</strong>","<strong><code>Ctrl + Shift + Del</code></strong>","Slices out the highlighted horizontal time selection across ALL tracks, pulling all downstream clips, locators, and automation left to close the gap.","Trimming an overextended 16-bar Verse down to an 8-bar radio verse in 1 second."],["<strong>Duplicate Time</strong>","<strong><code>Ctrl + Shift + D</code></strong>","Duplicates the highlighted time selection across ALL tracks and inserts the duplicate immediately after the selection, pushing subsequent material to the right.","Doubling an 8-bar Chorus into a 16-bar extended Climax Chorus with identical automation."],["<strong>Cut Time</strong>","<strong><code>Ctrl + Shift + X</code></strong>","Cuts the selected global time block across all tracks to the clipboard and closes the timeline gap.","Moving an entire Pre-Chorus block to a different location on the ruler."]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">BEFORE Duplicate Time (Bars 25–32 Highlighted):\n[ Verse 1 (8b) ] [ CHORUS 1 (8b) ] [ Verse 2 (8b) ]\n\nAFTER Ctrl + Shift + D on Bars 25–32:\n[ Verse 1 (8b) ] [ CHORUS 1 (8b) ] [ CHORUS 1 (8b) ] [ Verse 2 (8b) ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Highlight an 8-bar region across 4 tracks, press <code>Ctrl + Shift + D</code>, and verify that all tracks, automation lanes, and locators duplicate and shift downstream material perfectly."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-5","title":"9.1.5 — Slicing and Consolidating Timeline Audio","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.5 — Slicing and Consolidating Timeline Audio","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Execute surgical waveform editing using Split and Consolidate commands.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Split Command (Ctrl + E)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Position the cursor anywhere on an audio or MIDI clip (or highlight a range).","Press <strong><code>Ctrl + E</code></strong>. Live instantly divides the clip into two independent containers at that exact time boundary.","<em>Production Utility</em>: Slicing out the final beat of Bar 8 on all tracks to create an absolute 1-beat silence micro-gap before a drop downbeat."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. The Consolidate Command (Ctrl + J)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Splicing and rearranging multiple audio fragments creates cluttered, CPU-heavy project states with dozens of separate audio headers.","Highlight the spliced audio regions across time  Press <strong><code>Ctrl + J</code></strong>.","Live renders a brand-new, continuous audio file spanning the exact selected time boundaries, baking all clip gains, pitch transpositions, and crossfades into a single pristine audio asset."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Slice an 8-bar audio file into 8 single-bar pieces (<code>Ctrl + E</code>), reverse bars 3 and 7 (<code>R</code>), highlight all 8 bars, and press <code>Ctrl + J</code>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-6","title":"9.1.6 — Linked-Track Editing in Live 12 Standard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.6 — Linked-Track Editing in Live 12 Standard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Maintain multi-channel phase coherence and multi-mic alignment during timeline comping and editing.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Multi-Track Phase Integrity","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When recording stereo acoustic guitars (Left/Right mics), multi-mic drum kits (Kick In, Kick Out, Snare Top, Snare Bottom, Overheads), or double-tracked vocal stems:"},{"type":"list","items":["Slicing or time-stretching Track 1 independently of Track 2 destroys the phase relationship between microphones, causing hollow, comb-filtered acoustic cancellations."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Linking Tracks Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Hold <code>Ctrl</code> and click the track headers of all related channels (e.g., <code>GTR_MIC_L</code> and <code>GTR_MIC_R</code>).","Right-click any highlighted track header  Select <strong>Link Tracks</strong>.","A linked-track icon appears on each track header.","Any split (<code>Ctrl + E</code>), time trim, clip movement, or Take Lane comp selection performed on <code>GTR_MIC_L</code> is automatically and identically mirrored on <code>GTR_MIC_R</code>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">graph TD\n    UserEdit[\"User performs Split (Ctrl+E) on Track 1\"]\n    subgraph Linked Track Group\n        T1[\"Track 1: GTR_LEFT (Edit Applied)\"]\n        T2[\"Track 2: GTR_RIGHT (Mirrored Instantly)\"]\n    end\n    UserEdit --&gt; T1\n    T1 -.-&gt;|Phase-Locked Mirror| T2</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Link two audio tracks, make a split on Track 1, and verify that Track 2 splits at the exact identical sample position."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-7","title":"9.1.7 — Fades and Equal-Power Crossfades as Arrangement Glue","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.7 — Fades and Equal-Power Crossfades as Arrangement Glue","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Eliminate zero-crossing DC-offset clicks and blend overlapping audio files.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Physics of Zero-Crossing Discontinuities","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When an audio clip is sliced abruptly at a non-zero amplitude point, the loudspeaker driver is forced to instantaneously jump from a positive/negative voltage to zero volts. This produces an audible high-frequency transient click or pop (DC-offset discontinuity)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Non-Destructive Fade Handles","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Hover the cursor over the top-left or top-right boundary of any timeline audio clip.","Drag the <strong>Fade In</strong> or <strong>Fade Out</strong> handle inwards to create a 4–10 ms equal-power fade.","When two adjacent audio clips touch or overlap, drag the fade handle across the boundary to create an <strong>Equal-Power Crossfade</strong>, smoothly blending the decay of Clip A into the attack of Clip B without any perceived dip in RMS volume."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Create a crossfade between two overlapping ambient audio textures and adjust the curve tension to create a seamless acoustic transition."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-8","title":"9.1.8 — Consolidate Time to New Scene: The Reverse Direction","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.8 — Consolidate Time to New Scene: The Reverse Direction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Harvest linear arrangement sections back into Session View scenes (Manual §7.5).  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Bidirectional Workflow (Ableton Manual §7.5)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While beginners often jam in Session and record to Arrangement, professional producers frequently work in the <strong>reverse direction</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["You compose a complex, 64-bar linear arrangement on the ruler with comped vocal takes and intricate automation.","You then harvest those linear sections back into modular Session scenes for live stage performance or DJ remixing:","Highlight an 8-bar linear section across all tracks in Arrangement View (e.g., Bars 33.1.1 to 41.1.1, Chorus 1).","Right-click the highlighted time selection  Select <strong>Consolidate Time to New Scene</strong>.","Live automatically creates a new scene in Session View containing newly bounced, quantized clip loops of those exact tracks."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Highlight Bars 1–8 across 3 tracks in Arrangement View, select <em>Consolidate Time to New Scene</em>, and verify that the newly generated scene appears in Session View."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-9","title":"9.1.9 — Why This File Is NOT Deliverable 1","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.9 — Why This File Is NOT Deliverable 1","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Enforce strict musical independence between Deliverables 1 and 2.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Non-Duplication Attestation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In this academy curriculum, <strong>Deliverable 1</strong> and <strong>Deliverable 2</strong> represent two distinct musical compositions authored through two divergent compositional mindsets:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Deliverable 1</strong>: Conceived as non-linear modular loops, performed live via Launchkey MK4 pad triggers.","<strong>Deliverable 2</strong>: Conceived as a linear horizontal narrative, written from Bar 1 left-to-right on the ruler with comped take lanes.","<em>Rule</em>: Opening your Deliverable 1 Live Set and exporting the recorded arrangement pass is a direct violation of course standards. Deliverable 2 must start from a clean, blank Arrangement template with unique tempo, chords, and melodies."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-1-10","title":"9.1.10 — 32 Empty Bars with Locators Only (Gate D Scaffold)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.10 — 32 Empty Bars with Locators Only (Gate D Scaffold)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Construct the structural foundation for Gate D Certification.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Gate D Scaffold Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before recording any musical notes for Gate D:"},{"type":"list","items":["Open a blank Live Set: <code>Gate_D_Arrangement_Proof.als</code>.","Set project tempo (e.g., 124.0 BPM).","Insert 4 named Locators:","<code>01_Intro</code> (Bar 1.1.1)","<code>02_Verse</code> (Bar 9.1.1)","<code>03_Pre_Chorus</code> (Bar 17.1.1)","<code>04_Chorus</code> (Bar 25.1.1)","Verify that the 32-bar timeline span is visually divided into four distinct 8-bar quadrants."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-1","title":"9.2.1 — Bar 1 Is a Decision: The Linear Writing Mindset","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.1 — Bar 1 Is a Decision: The Linear Writing Mindset","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Establish forward momentum by committing to the opening sonic event.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Psychological Trap of the Empty Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Session View, the producer loops an isolated 8-bar groove without worrying about how the song starts or ends. On the Arrangement ruler, every bar has a fixed past and future."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Principle of Commitment</strong>: Bar 1 Beat 1 is an artistic decision. It establishes the key center, acoustic intimacy, and expectation for the entire piece.","Avoid staring at an empty timeline. Place an immediate sonic event on 1.1.1 (a sustained Rhodes chord, a vinyl crackle noise floor, or an unfiltered acoustic guitar strum) and immediately begin authoring forward in time toward the Verse."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-2","title":"9.2.2 — The Three Starting Doors on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.2 — The Three Starting Doors on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Choose one of three systematic entry methods for linear composition.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Methodological Starting Points","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">graph TD\n    Start[\"Linear Writing Strategy (Bar 1)\"]\n    Start --&gt; D1[\"Door 1: The Drum Spine&lt;br&gt;Lay down a 64-bar guide kick/percussion track along the entire timeline to establish tempo and sectional pulse.\"]\n    Start --&gt; D2[\"Door 2: The Continuous Chord Progression&lt;br&gt;Record an unbroken keyboard or guitar pass from Intro through Outro to lock harmonic movement.\"]\n    Start --&gt; D3[\"Door 3: The Chorus First Architecture&lt;br&gt;Compose the high-energy Chorus at Bar 25 first, then reverse-engineer the sparse Verse and rising Pre-Chorus.\"]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Door 1 (Rhythm First)</strong>: Ideal for club genres (House, Techno, DnB) where drum density dictates section identity.","<strong>Door 2 (Harmony First)</strong>: Ideal for Pop, R&amp;B, and Indie songwriting where chord cadence guides vocal phrasing.","<strong>Door 3 (Peak First)</strong>: The universal cure for weak verses. When you know how huge your Chorus sounds at Bar 25, you know exactly how sparse Verse 1 must be at Bar 9."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Select Door 3: Compose an 8-bar Chorus at Bar 25, then write an 8-bar Verse at Bar 9 that uses exactly half the instruments."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-3","title":"9.2.3 — Recording Long MIDI and Audio Takes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.3 — Recording Long MIDI and Audio Takes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Capture expressive, continuous instrumental performances across the timeline.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Continuous Performance vs. Loop Copying","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Copying and pasting a 2-bar MIDI chord clip 32 times creates static, robotic music. "},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Continuous Take Execution</strong>:","Arm Track 3 (<code>KEYS_RHODES</code>).","Engage the metronome (<code>C</code>).","Press <strong>Arrangement Record (<code>●</code>)</strong>.","Perform an unbroken 64-bar pass from Bar 1 to Bar 65.","Play natural dynamic accents, slight rhythmic variations, and expressive chord inversions on the Launchkey MK4 velocity-sensitive keys as sections transition."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-4","title":"9.2.4 — Live 12 Standard Take Lanes Comping (Ch. 21)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.4 — Live 12 Standard Take Lanes Comping (Ch. 21)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Master multi-take loop recording and composite take assembly in Live 12 Standard.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Take Lane Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Comping allows a producer to record multiple continuous passes over a looped section and assemble the single best performance into the Main Track Lane:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">graph TD\n    MasterLane[\"Main Track Lane: COMPOSITE MASTER OUTPUT\"]\n    TL1[\"Take Lane 1: Pass 1 (Bars 9–16)\"]\n    TL2[\"Take Lane 2: Pass 2 (Bars 9–16)\"]\n    TL3[\"Take Lane 3: Pass 3 (Bars 9–16)\"]\n    TL4[\"Take Lane 4: Pass 4 (Bars 9–16)\"]\n    \n    TL1 --&gt;|Phrase A Promoted| MasterLane\n    TL3 --&gt;|Phrase B Promoted| MasterLane\n    TL2 --&gt;|Phrase C Promoted| MasterLane</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Step-by-Step Comping Workflow","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set the Loop Brace (<code>Ctrl + L</code>) over the target section (e.g., Bars 9–17 for Verse 1).","Arm the audio track (e.g., <code>LEAD_VOCAL</code>).","Press <strong>Record (<code>●</code>)</strong> and perform 4 to 6 continuous passes without stopping playback.","Stop recording. Right-click the track header  Select <strong>Show Take Lanes (<code>Ctrl + Alt + U</code>)</strong>.","Live displays each recorded pass in its own dedicated sub-lane below the main track.","Click the <strong>Audition Button (Speaker Icon)</strong> on each Take Lane to solo individual takes.","Activate the <strong>Draw Tool (<code>B</code>)</strong> or click-and-drag with the mouse cursor to highlight the best vocal phrase in Take Lane 1.","Press <strong><code>Enter</code></strong> (or let go of the mouse). Live immediately promotes that exact phrase up into the Main Track Lane.","Highlight phrase 2 in Take Lane 3 and phrase 3 in Take Lane 2.","Automatic crossfades are created at all splice boundaries. The Main Track Lane now plays a flawless composite master take."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Record 3 audio takes over an 8-bar loop, open Take Lanes (<code>Ctrl + Alt + U</code>), and assemble a composite master take from all 3 lanes."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-5","title":"9.2.5 — Overdubbing a Counter-Line with MIDI Overdub","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.5 — Overdubbing a Counter-Line with MIDI Overdub","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Layer expressive melodic motifs onto existing MIDI clips on the timeline.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Non-Destructive MIDI Layering","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Arrangement View, engaging standard recording over an existing MIDI clip overwrites the clip contents.","<strong>The Overdub Workflow</strong>:","Click the <strong>MIDI Overdub Button (<code>+</code> icon)</strong> in Live's top Control Bar.","Start playback over Verse 2 (Bar 49).","Play an arpeggiated counter-melody or percussive syncopation on the Launchkey MK4.","Live writes the new MIDI notes directly into the existing clip container without erasing the underlying chords."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-6","title":"9.2.6 — Novation Launchkey MK4 Hardware Operations on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.6 — Novation Launchkey MK4 Hardware Operations on the Ruler","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Operate Arrangement transport, overdub, undo, and loop brace controls directly from hardware.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Hardware Control Mapping","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 provides dedicated transport and recording controls that eliminate mouse dependency during linear writing:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Record (●)</code></strong>: Toggles Arrangement recording on/off.","<strong><code>Play (▶) / Stop (■)</code></strong>: Starts and stops global timeline playback.","<strong><code>Loop</code> Button</strong>: Toggles the Arrangement Loop Brace on and off.","<strong><code>Undo</code></strong>: Instantly deletes the last recorded take or edit without touching the keyboard.","<strong><code>Capture MIDI</code></strong>: Press after playing an unrecorded improvisational pass on the ruler to instantiate a new MIDI clip containing the played notes."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-7","title":"9.2.7 — Automation Lanes as Arrangement Devices","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.7 — Automation Lanes as Arrangement Devices","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Draw parameter automation curves to sculpt sectional contrast and forward motion.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Toggling Automation Mode (A)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Press <strong><code>A</code></strong> to toggle Arrangement Automation Mode. Track lanes expand to reveal automation envelopes overlaid directly on top of audio waveforms and MIDI notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Structural Automation Examples","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Speaker On/Off (Track Mute)</strong>: Draw mute automation to silence the kick drum on Bar 24 Beat 4 (the pre-drop micro-gap).","<strong>Auto Filter Frequency</strong>: Click the <code>Frequency</code> knob on an Auto Filter device  Live automatically assigns the automation lane to that parameter. Draw an upward slope from 200 Hz to 18 kHz across Bars 17–24 (Pre-Chorus).","<strong>Reverb Send A</strong>: Draw a sudden +6 dB spike on Beat 4 of Bar 24 to wash the final snare hit into a massive spatial splash."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Press <code>A</code>, select Track 1 Auto Filter Cutoff, draw an upward ramp across 8 bars, and hold <code>Alt</code> to curve the line."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-8","title":"9.2.8 — Duplicating and Positioning Chorus Blocks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.8 — Duplicating and Positioning Chorus Blocks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Position and adapt repeated structural sections across the timeline.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Macro-Timeline Placement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Once Chorus 1 (Bars 25–40) is fully arranged:"},{"type":"list","items":["Highlight Bars 25.1.1 to 41.1.1 across all tracks.","Hold <strong><code>Alt</code></strong> and drag the highlighted block to Bar 57.1.1 (the start of Chorus 2), or press <strong><code>Ctrl + D</code></strong> to duplicate downstream.","Reposition the downstream locators to maintain strict alignment with the new block."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-9","title":"9.2.9 — Varying the Final Chorus: The Climax Rule","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.9 — Varying the Final Chorus: The Climax Rule","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Ensure that subsequent chorus and drop sections exhibit structural escalation.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The 100% Macro-Energy Invariant","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In professional popular and electronic music, <strong>Chorus 2 / Drop 2 must NEVER be an exact clone of Chorus 1 / Drop 1</strong>:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Structural Parameter","Chorus 1 (Bar 25)","Final Chorus Climax (Bar 65)"],"rows":[["<strong>Duration</strong>","8 to 16 Bars","16 to 24 Bars (Double Chorus + Tag)"],["<strong>Percussion Density</strong>","Kick + Snare + 8th Closed Hats","Kick + Snare + 16th Ride Cymbals + Tambourine"],["<strong>Melodic Octave</strong>","Lead in Octave 4 (C4–C5)","Lead doubled in Octave 5 (C5–C6) with saw layer"],["<strong>Vocal Arrangement</strong>","Centered Dry Lead Vocal","Lead Vocal + Stereo Doublers + High Falsetto Ad-Libs"]],"caption":null}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-2-10","title":"9.2.10 — Authoring the Section Bar-Count Sheet","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.10 — Authoring the Section Bar-Count Sheet","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Construct formal bar-count documentation to verify proportional arrangement balance.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Bar-Count Specification Matrix","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Document the exact bar allocation for Deliverable 2 in <code>DELIVERABLE_2_MAP.txt</code>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">===================================================================\nDELIVERABLE 2: LINEAR ARRANGEMENT BAR-COUNT SPECIFICATION\nTEMPO: 124.00 BPM | KEY CENTER: A Minor | TOTAL BARS: 96 Bars (3m 05s)\n===================================================================\n- 01_Intro:            Bars 1–8   (8 bars)  [Filtered Rhodes + Vinyl atmosphere]\n- 02_Verse_1:          Bars 9–24  (16 bars) [Sparse Kick + Rimshot + Comped Vocal Take]\n- 03_Pre_Chorus_1:     Bars 25–32 (8 bars)  [Sub bass cut + Snare roll riser + Filter sweep]\n- 04_Chorus_1:         Bars 33–48 (16 bars) [Full 4-on-floor + Comped Lead Hook + Wide Chords]\n- 05_Verse_2:          Bars 49–56 (8 bars)  [Short verse + 16th Shakers + Bass locked]\n- 06_Bridge_Breakdown: Bars 57–64 (8 bars)  [Acoustic piano departure + Drums muted]\n- 07_Final_Chorus:     Bars 65–88 (24 bars) [Double Chorus + Ride Cymbal + Top-Octave Lead + Ad-Libs]\n- 08_Outro:            Bars 89–96 (8 bars)  [Subtractive decay + Final chord fade]\n===================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-1","title":"9.3.1 — Marking Every 8-Bar Boundary on a Reference Track","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.1 — Marking Every 8-Bar Boundary on a Reference Track","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Analyze commercial track pacing by mapping 8-bar structural gridlines.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Reference Track Structural Auditing","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Import a lossless, uncompressed commercial reference track onto Track 1 (<code>REF</code>).","Route <code>REF</code> output directly to <code>Ext. Out</code> (bypassing master bus processing).","Align the first downbeat of the song to Bar <code>1.1.1</code>.","Drop Locators at every single 8-bar boundary (Bars 1, 9, 17, 25, 33, 41, 49, 57, 65...).","Play through the song and document:","<em>What entered on Bar 9?</em> (Closed hi-hats).","<em>What left on Bar 17?</em> (Kick drum dropped out for the build).","<em>What happened on Bar 24 Beat 4?</em> (1 beat of total silence).","<em>What hit on Bar 25 Beat 1?</em> (Massive drop with sub-bass and crash cymbal)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-2","title":"9.3.2 — Drawing the Macro-Energy Curve First","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.2 — Drawing the Macro-Energy Curve First","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Plot the sectional energy percentage profile prior to timeline authoring.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Energy Curve Formulation (Beat Kitchen)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Energy in electronic music is a mathematical relationship between <strong>Dynamic Range (RMS/LUFS)</strong>, <strong>Spectral Density (Bandwidth)</strong>, <strong>Rhythmic Subdivision Frequency</strong>, and <strong>Harmonic Tension</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">graph LR\n    Intro[\"Intro&lt;br&gt;40%\"] --&gt; V1[\"Verse 1&lt;br&gt;55%\"]\n    V1 --&gt; Pre[\"Pre-Chorus&lt;br&gt;75%\"]\n    Pre --&gt; C1[\"Chorus 1&lt;br&gt;90%\"]\n    C1 --&gt; V2[\"Verse 2&lt;br&gt;60%\"]\n    V2 --&gt; Bridge[\"Bridge&lt;br&gt;35%\"]\n    Bridge --&gt; Pre2[\"Build 2&lt;br&gt;80%\"]\n    Pre2 --&gt; Climax[\"Final Chorus&lt;br&gt;100%\"]\n    Climax --&gt; Outro[\"Outro&lt;br&gt;25%\"]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If an arrangement maintains a constant 85% energy line from Bar 1 to Bar 96, the listener's auditory system experiences acoustic fatigue, and the chorus ceases to feel impactful."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-3","title":"9.3.3 — Buildup Tools as Form: Density, Filters, and Riser Clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.3 — Buildup Tools as Form: Density, Filters, and Riser Clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Synthesize high-tension risers and sweeps strictly using Live 12 Standard devices.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. White Noise Riser Architecture in Live 12 Standard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do not hunt for third-party riser samples. Build a native white noise riser engine:"},{"type":"list","items":["Load <code>Analog</code> or <code>Drift</code> onto an Audio/MIDI track (<code>FX_RISER</code>).","Set Oscillator 1 to <strong>White Noise</strong> (100% level).","Insert Live’s stock <code>Auto Filter</code> device after the synth.","Set Filter Type to <strong>Band-Pass (BP)</strong> with 12 dB/oct slope and Resonance at 45%.","On the Arrangement timeline across Bars 25–32, draw an automation line sweeping the Cutoff Frequency upward from 150 Hz to 16 kHz.","Insert Live’s stock <code>Delay</code> (Ping-Pong, 8th-note dotted) and <code>Reverb</code> (40% Wet) to wash the noise across the stereo field."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-4","title":"9.3.4 — Low-End Evacuation: Sub Leaves in Build, Detonates on Drop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.4 — Low-End Evacuation: Sub Leaves in Build, Detonates on Drop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Master the psychoacoustics of pre-drop low-frequency clearing.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Headroom Physics of the Drop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Human hearing judges loudness relative to preceding acoustic events:"},{"type":"list","items":["If sub-bass (30–80 Hz) is blasting at -3 dBFS during the entire buildup, the drop downbeat has nowhere to go dynamically.","<strong>The Evacuation Rule</strong>:","Across the final 4 to 8 bars of the buildup (Bars 25–32), insert an <code>Auto Filter</code> on the Bass track and Master Bass bus.","Automate a high-pass filter sweeping upward from 30 Hz to 350 Hz.","Mute the kick drum for the final 4 bars.","When the sub-bass and kick detonate unfiltered on Bar 33 Beat 1.1.1, the acoustic contrast makes the drop sound twice as massive."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-5","title":"9.3.5 — Breakdown: Resisting the Urge to Overfill","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.5 — Breakdown: Resisting the Urge to Overfill","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Protect dynamic valley integrity to maximize downstream climax impact.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Protecting the Dynamic Valley","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The mid-song breakdown (Bars 57–64) is the emotional pivot of the song:"},{"type":"list","items":["Strip away all drums, percussive loops, and sub-bass.","Feature only an intimate acoustic piano, a filtered synth pad, or a dry vocal whisper.","The deeper the dynamic valley (35% energy), the more explosive the final chorus climax (100%) will feel when it arrives."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-6","title":"9.3.6 — Subtractive Transitions: Clearing Phrase Edges","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.6 — Subtractive Transitions: Clearing Phrase Edges","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Slice out audio elements at phrase boundaries to signal structural arrivals.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The 1-Bar Subtractive Cut","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Bar 8 of an 8-bar section (e.g., Bar 24, the final bar before the drop):"},{"type":"list","items":["Highlight and slice (<code>Ctrl + E</code>) the hi-hats, shakers, and background synth pads.","Press <strong><code>0</code> (Deactivate Clip)</strong> to silence them.","Leaving only a solitary snare roll or vocal pickup signals to the listener that the section is resolving."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-7","title":"9.3.7 — Additive Transitions: Reverse Crashes, Fills, and Turnarounds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.7 — Additive Transitions: Reverse Crashes, Fills, and Turnarounds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Execute forward-motion turnaround mechanics into section downbeats.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The 9 Canonical Turnaround Levers (The Art of Turnarounds)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A Turnaround occurs strictly on the final 1 or 2 bars of an 8-bar block (Bar 8 or Bars 7–8):"},{"type":"table","headers":["#","Turnaround Technique","Live 12 Standard Technical Execution"],"rows":[["1","<strong>The Drum Fill</strong>","Program a syncopated tom/snare roll cascading down in pitch on Bar 8."],["2","<strong>The Micro-Gap (Silence)</strong>","Mute ALL tracks on Beat 4 of Bar 8 right before the downbeat."],["3","<strong>The Pre-Drop Vocal</strong>","Insert a short dry vocal phrase (\"Drop it\", \"Let's go\") in the 1-beat silence."],["4","<strong>The Reverse Cymbal Swell</strong>","Reverse a crash cymbal sample (<code>R</code> key) ending precisely on Bar 9.1.1."],["5","<strong>The Low-Pass Sweep</strong>","Automate <code>Auto Filter</code> low-pass cutoff sweeping down to 400 Hz on Bar 8, snapping wide open on Bar 9."],["6","<strong>The Pitch Dive / Tapestop</strong>","Automate clip transpose down -12 st across Bar 8 Beat 4."],["7","<strong>The Reverb Splash</strong>","Automate Return A Send from - dB to +6 dB on the final snare hit, cutting dry on Beat 4."],["8","<strong>The Sub Drop</strong>","A pure sine wave sweeping downward from 120 Hz to 30 Hz on Bar 8."],["9","<strong>The Harmonic Pivot</strong>","End Bar 8 on an unresolved dominant 7th (V7) chord."]],"caption":null}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-8","title":"9.3.8 — Second Peak Must Differ: The Climax Invariant","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.8 — Second Peak Must Differ: The Climax Invariant","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Enforce variance and escalation across secondary peaks.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. The Climax Variance Audit","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Inspect Chorus 2 / Drop 2:"},{"type":"list","items":["[x] Is there an added high-frequency layer (ride cymbal or tambourine)?","[x] Is the melodic lead doubled in a higher octave?","[x] Are vocal ad-libs present?","If all three answers are NO, the arrangement violates the Climax Invariant."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-9","title":"9.3.9 — Automation Curve Tension vs. Vertical Cliffs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.9 — Automation Curve Tension vs. Vertical Cliffs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Eliminate digital zero-crossing clicks using curved automation envelopes.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Curving Automation Segments","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Automation Mode (<code>A</code>), hover over any linear automation segment between two breakpoints.","Hold <strong><code>Alt</code></strong> (Windows) and drag up or down to bend the line into an exponential or logarithmic curve:","<strong>Concave (Slow start, steep end)</strong>: Holds suspense until the final beat.","<strong>Convex (Fast start, sustained high)</strong>: Opens energy immediately.","Never draw a perfectly vertical 90^ automation cliff directly on a transient boundary, as it can cause DAC buffer pops. Curve the line smoothly through the downbeat."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l9-3-10","title":"9.3.10 — 64 Bars That Are NOT a Flat Energy Line","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.10 — 64 Bars That Are NOT a Flat Energy Line","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Audit the completed 64-bar linear arrangement against the master mistake canon.  "}]},{"type":"step","title":"1. Final Linear Arrangement Verification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play your 64-bar arrangement from Bar 1 to Bar 65:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Are named Locators pinned to every section downbeat?","[ ] Does energy modulate dynamically between 35% and 100%?","[ ] Is there a verified comped take lane on at least one audio track?","[ ] Does the arrangement feature an automated turnaround and micro-gap before the drop?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-gate-d","title":"D — Competency Assessment Checklist","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"D — Competency Assessment Checklist","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To achieve <strong>Gate D Certification</strong> and unlock the Linear Arrangement Capstone, the learner must verify the following competencies in Live 12 Standard:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] <strong>1. Linear Timeline Scaffolding</strong>: Minimum 32-bar contiguous linear composition authored directly on the Arrangement ruler with explicitly named Locators (<code>01_Intro</code>, <code>02_Verse</code>, <code>03_Pre_Chorus</code>, <code>04_Chorus</code>).","[ ] <strong>2. Independent Origin</strong>: Formal written attestation in <code>GATE_D_PROOF.txt</code> certifying that this timeline is an original composition, NOT a recorded pass or duplicate of Deliverable 1.","[ ] <strong>3. Comped Take Lane Proof</strong>: At least one audio track displaying active Take Lanes (<code>Ctrl + Alt + U</code>) with an assembled composite master take promoted to the main lane.","[ ] <strong>4. Automated Turnaround &amp; Micro-Gap</strong>: A turnaround implemented on Bar 24 containing a white noise riser sweep and a 1-beat total silence micro-gap before the Chorus downbeat."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
